					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dapwndaz" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2084215); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2084215&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608608560" alt="daPWNDAZ" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2084215)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dapwndaz" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2084215); return false;" >daPWNDAZ</a></span> <span class="odate time_1430326800 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">29 Apr 2015 17:00</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2282242">
									

<p>The concept of this skip is pretty decent. However, there are a few things that I see that could use some fixing.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Incoming</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Poof</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Starting off, it says in the containment procedure (talking about the discovery of new instances) to see the discovery log for related incidents. First off, it isn't formatted in a log template. You can either change the &quot;discovery log&quot; at the end to incident reports, or even change it into an actual log.</p>
<p>Additionally, it never states what the personnel should do in the event that 1120 becomes active. Given that later in the article you find out that diamond-tipped tools can pierce the rock, shouldn't there be more than just saying how many personnel should be armed with guns with diamond bullets? In the containment procedures, it says nothing about any &quot;active&quot; state. If I were in charge of watching some rock people, I would want to know what I should do if one of them starts moving. Perhaps just expand on what procedures the guards should follow (it could even be &quot;shoot them,&quot; so long as there's something like that included.</p>
<p>Then it comes to the walls themselves. You state that instances are capable of destroying walls, so what does bone-marrow concrete add in security? Unless humans have developed titanium bones recently, I don't see how a wall like that would stop it. Perhaps add a clause somewhere that states (or even implies) that they have an aversion to bones and rocks, perhaps saying that they think the walls are their own people, and so won't destroy it.</p>
<p>In the decryption, the line &quot;at the current time, all instances of SCP-1120 are in a dormant state.&quot; It should read &quot;current time of writing.&quot; Why? Because, as it currently stands, it gives no reference to time. It implies that the instances are always dormant. Continuing in the path of dormancy, what makes them dormant? Does them touching a person make them dormant? Or do they continue attacking people, even after they &quot;tag&quot; a person? As for the new instances generated, are they dormant or active?</p>
<p>Lastly, the addendum. This is just for aesthetics. I've seen some documents that don't label the addendum, and I've seen some that have. My opinion is that it makes the document look more professional when it is labeled. Such as <strong>Addendum 1120-a</strong>, but it could be 1120-b or 1120-1, however you feel to classify it. Then, there's the set of italics set within the addendum. You could keep it there, or you could move it outside the body and have it where that block is entirely the document itself, and no notes attached.</p>
<p>The overall content is decent, but I thin I'll keep it at a no-vote for now.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

